  "preamble": "In Southern Min (Hokkien, Teochew), Pu-Xian and Hainan Min, nasal codas after open vowels disappeared in the vernacular layer, leaving the preceding vowel nasalized, so Middle Chinese -am surfaces as [ã]. The literary (reading) layer keeps the coda [-m], which gives doublets such as Hokkien saⁿ / sam for 三 'three'. After close vowels the coda is kept, as in 南 Jieyang nam.",
